    "problem_statement": "NameError when accessing environment variables with default values\n\n#### Description\nIt seems there's an issue when trying to access environment variables with default values using the `environs` library. The problem arises when attempting to retrieve a variable that isn't set in the environment, and a default value is provided. This results in a `NameError`.\n\n#### Steps/Code to Reproduce\n\n```python\nfrom environs import Env\n\nenv = Env()\n\n# Attempt to access a non-existent environment variable with a default value\ntry:\n    value = env.str('NON_EXISTENT_VAR', 'default_value')\n    print(f\"Value: {value}\")\nexcept NameError as e:\n    print(f\"Error: {e}\")\n```\n\n#### Expected Results\nThe code should print:\n```\nValue: default_value\n```\n\n#### Actual Results\nThe code raises a `NameError`:\n```\nNameError: name 'load_default' is not defined\n```\n\n#### Versions\n- Python: 3.10.16\n- environs: [version]\n- Operating System: [OS details]\n\nIf anyone has insights on this, it would be greatly appreciated!"
